I read the code of `socket.io` and learned that the `stringify` process is done by `socket.io`, not Cypress. 

The user-given data is sent to server with `runner` below:

https://github.com/cypress-io/cypress/blob/1e7a3faa4c26bea5aea12be4f5eb023235c3b1a4/packages/runner/src/lib/event-manager.js#L367-L371

When you check the `args` above, `body` is still `Blob` object.

And the data passed to `socket.io` is passed to [`socket.io`'s `emit()`](https://github.com/socketio/socket.io/blob/master/lib/socket.ts#L191-L217) to [`packet()`](https://github.com/socketio/socket.io/blob/master/lib/socket.ts#L281-L288) to [`socket.io-client`'s `_packet()`](https://github.com/socketio/socket.io-client/blob/2edf0f308c8b969928124d02e81e21c456f736a7/lib/manager.ts#L672-L679), which calls the `encode()` of [`socket.io-parser`'s `encode()`](https://github.com/socketio/socket.io-parser/blob/af1b23ca85fb64a0d7a050abd0362c5f632ce429/lib/index.ts#L44-L57) below:

```js
  public encode(obj: Packet) {
    debug("encoding packet %j", obj);


    if (obj.type === PacketType.EVENT || obj.type === PacketType.ACK) {
      if (hasBinary(obj)) { // <= this line
        obj.type =
          obj.type === PacketType.EVENT
            ? PacketType.BINARY_EVENT
            : PacketType.BINARY_ACK;
        return this.encodeAsBinary(obj);
      }
    }
    return [this.encodeAsString(obj)];
  }
```

It calls `hasBinary()` and it calls the `isBinary` function you referenced above. And I checked that [`hasBinary`](https://github.com/socketio/socket.io-parser/blob/af1b23ca85fb64a0d7a050abd0362c5f632ce429/lib/is-binary.ts#L33-L66) ignores JavaScript objects that contain binary data as in our case. It only checks the entire data is binary or not. I'm not sure why they ignored this case, but I suspect it's because of the performance. As we all know, traversing tree is really expensive. 

As for our case, I guess we need to mimic the behavior of `encodeAsBinary()`. It might not be hard because we can reuse the functions in `socket.io-parser`. Or there might be other better solutions.

